  "interpretation": {
    "1_the_cohorts_do_not_share_a_score_scale": "Delaware's IMPAIRED mean score is 0.409, essentially identical to Pitt's HEALTHY mean of 0.406 and below Lu's HEALTHY mean of 0.471. A single cut-point therefore cannot mean the same thing in all three. This is the mechanism behind the specificity collapse reported in the locked external evaluation, and it is visible without touching Lu at all.",
    "2_the_deployed_threshold": "At 0.367: Pitt 0.856/0.506, Delaware 0.569/0.659, Lu 0.962/0.333. The worst sensitivity across cohorts is 0.569 -- i.e. at the deployed threshold the Delaware cohort does not meet even the 0.75 screening floor the threshold was chosen to satisfy on the pooled development set.",
    "3_what_each_cohort_would_choose_alone": "At a 0.75 floor the three cohorts choose 0.510 (Pitt), 0.291 (Delaware) and 0.607 (Lu) -- a spread of 0.32 on a 0-1 scale. Each is defensible in its own cohort (specificity 0.770, 0.376, 0.778). There is no single number near all three.",
    "4_the_joint_rule_is_the_wrong_shape_for_this_problem": "Forcing one threshold to satisfy every cohort drives it down to 0.286 / 0.266 / 0.253 and the worst specificity to 0.185 / 0.074 / 0.037. A screening tool that clears 4% of healthy people is not a screening tool. The finding is not 'pick 0.286' -- it is that a single global threshold is the wrong instrument, and per-deployment threshold setting on local data is required.",
    "5_recalibration_cannot_rescue_it_either": "The two development cohorts are miscalibrated in OPPOSITE directions -- Pitt slope 1.516, Delaware slope 0.823 -- so the single intercept-slope map fitted on their pooled out-of-fold predictions (slope 1.289, intercept 0.138) improves Pitt (1.516 -> 1.176, Brier 0.181 -> 0.177) and makes Delaware WORSE (0.823 -> 0.638, Brier 0.220 -> 0.222). On Lu, which is genuinely out-of-sample for the fit, the slope moves toward 1 (1.886 -> 1.464) but the intercept moves further from 0 (-0.864 -> -1.066) and the Brier score worsens (0.179 -> 0.182): the model already over-predicts on Lu (mean 0.586 against an observed rate of 0.491) and the development-fitted map pushes scores further up, to 0.627. AUC is unchanged everywhere, as a monotone map requires.",
    "6_what_this_supports": "Per-deployment threshold and calibration setting on local data, and the staged design recorded in the future-work section: development pool -> a dedicated calibration cohort -> freeze everything -> ONE evaluation on a corpus that has never been touched."
  }
